    <h1>Raven Nesting Behaviour</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Sites, season, pair bonds, and breeding biology</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Ravens nest on <strong>cliff ledges and tall trees</strong>, beginning as early as February in northern latitudes — unusually early for a corvid. The nest is a large stick platform reused annually. Clutch: 3–7 eggs. Incubation: 20–21 days (female only). Fledging: 35–42 days. Young remain dependent on parents for several months post-fledging.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Nest Sites</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens show strong preference for elevated, inaccessible nest sites:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cliff ledges:</strong> The primary natural preference; cliff faces provide protection from predators and stable thermal conditions</li>
      <li><strong>Tall trees:</strong> Conifers and large deciduous trees; ravens build in the upper canopy</li>
      <li><strong>Pylons and transmission towers:</strong> Increasingly used in many parts of range; electrically problematic and sometimes managed</li>
      <li><strong>Bridges, buildings, quarry faces:</strong> Opportunistic use of human structures</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Nest site fidelity is high — pairs typically return to the same site year after year, with one study at a Welsh cliff site documenting the same location used for over 30 years (Dare 1986, cited in Boarman & Heinrich 1999). Some pairs maintain two or more alternate sites and switch between them.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Nest Structure</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Outer structure:</strong> Large platform of sticks and branches — can reach 60–120 cm diameter after years of annual additions</li>
      <li><strong>Inner cup:</strong> Lined with softer material — wool, hair, bark strips, dry grass, moss, feathers</li>
      <li><strong>Construction:</strong> Both sexes build; female does most of the lining</li>
      <li><strong>Time:</strong> Construction takes 1–3 weeks; re-lining of an existing nest takes less time</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The accumulated size of old nests can be extraordinary. Reused annually, some raven nests weigh tens of kilograms. The mass can occasionally cause structural problems on pylons.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a>,<a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Breeding Season and Timing</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens are early breeders relative to most birds at the same latitude:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nest building:</strong> January–February in UK and northern Europe; similar timing in North America</li>
      <li><strong>Egg laying:</strong> February–April (latitude-dependent)</li>
      <li><strong>Incubation:</strong> 20–21 days; female incubates, male provisiones her</li>
      <li><strong>Hatching:</strong> March–May</li>
      <li><strong>Fledging:</strong> 35–42 days after hatching</li>
      <li><strong>Post-fledging dependence:</strong> Several months — young ravens follow parents and beg food</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Early nesting means chicks are present in cold weather, requiring significant brooding. The adaptive advantage is that chicks fledge in late spring/early summer when food is becoming abundant. By the time winter arrives, young birds are independent.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Pair Bonds and Mate Fidelity</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens form long-term pair bonds, typically maintained until a partner dies:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Pairs defend territories year-round, not just during breeding</li>
      <li>Pair bond formation involves extended courtship — aerial displays, bill-to-bill contact, mutual preening, and food-sharing</li>
      <li>Divorce (voluntary pair dissolution while both partners survive) is rare but documented</li>
      <li>Widowed ravens typically re-pair with floater birds</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Both parents feed nestlings and fledglings, though the division of labour shifts: the male does most provisioning while the female broods. As chicks grow, both parents hunt and forage for the family.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a>,<a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Breeding Success and Chick Survival</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Long-term studies in Wales (Dare; cited in Boarman & Heinrich 1999) and North America provide breeding success data:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Mean clutch size: approximately 4–5 eggs in well-studied populations</li>
      <li>Hatching success: variable; infertility and early nest failure reduce it</li>
      <li>Fledging success: typically 1–3 chicks per successful nest</li>
      <li>First-year mortality: high — most deaths in the raven population occur before age 2</li>
      <li>Age at first breeding: typically 3–4 years; some individuals breed at 2</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Breeding pairs that hold good territories in productive habitats (e.g., near landfills or carcass-rich rangelands) show consistently higher chick output than pairs in marginal habitats.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Non-breeding Birds</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> A large proportion of the raven population in any area consists of non-breeding "floaters" — subadult and young adult birds that have not yet established territories:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>These birds form communal roosts and may number in the hundreds at good food sources</li>
      <li>They probe territorial pair boundaries and attempt to access territory resources</li>
      <li>They eventually acquire territories by inheriting vacant ones, displacing weakened residents, or establishing new territories at the periphery</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The two-tier social system (territorial pairs + floating non-breeders) is a key feature of Common Raven population ecology and explains much of the behaviour documented at large carcasses and food sources.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a>,<a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

